CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^.*operator 'shl' not defined.*$
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
Verifies that for an overloaded operator (`<<`) with both a member
function-template specialization and a non-template free
function (a `friend` in the enclosing class) viable, the
non-template wins per [over.match.best]/2 (paragraph 5/6).

Without the fix, `operator_is_overloaded` returned the first
viable member candidate (the template member instantiation)
without consulting the non-member candidate set; the body of
the template member then failed to typecheck against the
specific argument type that the friend was designed to handle.

This pattern occurs widely on CBMC's own `messaget`/`mstreamt`/
`eomt` triple in `src/util/message.h`: the friend `operator<<
(mstreamt &, eomt)` should win, but the member template
`operator<<(const T &)` was selected instead, with its
`static_cast<std::ostream &>(*this) << x` body then failing on
`x: eomt`.
